1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,245
You ride one without a second thought.

2
00:00:02,872 --> 00:00:05,013
The doors close, the numbers climb.

3
00:00:05,786 --> 00:00:07,994
Now imagine its ropes snap.

4
00:00:08,705 --> 00:00:10,778
You might expect to fall all the way down.

5
00:00:11,517 --> 00:00:16,366
Instead, you drop for a split second, and then you stop, within about a meter.

6
00:00:17,135 --> 00:00:17,984
What stopped you?

7
00:00:18,904 --> 00:00:19,725
Not a motor.

8
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,174
Not a computer.

9
00:00:21,791 --> 00:00:23,667
Your own fall pulled the brake.

10
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:26,057
And here is the strange part.

11
00:00:26,781 --> 00:00:28,711
That brake is built to slip.

12
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:32,850
And there is one danger this brake cannot stop at all.

13
00:00:33,483 --> 00:00:34,300
We will get to it.

14
00:00:35,671 --> 00:00:37,026
Snaps are rare.

15
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:39,815
Your car hangs on several ropes.

16
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:42,244
This brake is for the rare day.

17
00:00:43,619 --> 00:00:47,355
At the top of the shaft, there is a wheel that lifts nothing at all.

18
00:00:48,095 --> 00:00:51,759
You have never seen it, but it turns every time your car moves.

19
00:00:52,445 --> 00:00:53,689
So what is it for?

20
00:00:54,714 --> 00:00:56,531
Engineers call it the governor.

21
00:00:57,309 --> 00:01:01,643
A thin loop of rope runs over it, down to the bottom of the shaft and back up.

22
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:04,742
That rope is hooked to a lever on your car.

23
00:01:05,408 --> 00:01:09,858
So wherever your car goes, the rope goes too, and the wheel spins.

24
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:12,782
A faster car means a faster wheel.

25
00:01:13,964 --> 00:01:18,163
In a classic governor, weights inside the wheel are held in by springs.

26
00:01:18,797 --> 00:01:21,028
Spin it faster, and they swing outward.

27
00:01:21,695 --> 00:01:29,349
At a set speed, at least fifteen percent above your elevator's normal speed, they swing out far enough to catch, and the wheel locks.

28
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:31,159
Its rope stops.

29
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,085
It does not care why you are too fast.

30
00:01:34,619 --> 00:01:37,452
Snapped rope or anything else, it just locks.

31
00:01:38,494 --> 00:01:40,265
But your car does not stop.

32
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:44,055
It keeps falling, past a rope that is no longer moving.

33
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:46,968
And that lever is still hooked to the rope.

34
00:01:47,679 --> 00:01:50,104
The falling car pulls its own brake lever.

35
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:54,425
Many governors also flip a switch first, to cut the motor.

36
00:01:55,096 --> 00:01:57,659
But the brake itself is pure mechanics.

37
00:01:58,328 --> 00:02:04,616
The European standard for elevators says nothing electric, and no fluid or air pressure, may set it off.

38
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:10,101
Your car runs between steel guide rails that go the whole height of the shaft.

39
00:02:10,648 --> 00:02:11,707
You never see them.

40
00:02:12,452 --> 00:02:14,602
They run just outside the walls of your car.

41
00:02:15,488 --> 00:02:19,864
On each rail, the lever lifts steel wedges that close on it from both sides.

42
00:02:20,604 --> 00:02:23,788
And each wedge sits in a slot that narrows toward the top.

43
00:02:24,991 --> 00:02:26,769
Now watch what your fall does to them.

44
00:02:27,611 --> 00:02:30,346
Your car is falling, but the rail stands still.

45
00:02:31,057 --> 00:02:35,661
So the moment a wedge touches it, the rail holds the wedge back while the car drops.

46
00:02:36,405 --> 00:02:38,911
The wedge slides higher into the narrowing slot.

47
00:02:39,561 --> 00:02:41,953
The higher it goes, the harder it squeezes.

48
00:02:42,749 --> 00:02:43,688
Can you see the trick?

49
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:46,837
Your fall is feeding its own brake.

50
00:02:47,908 --> 00:02:51,375
So why not let it grab as hard as it can, and stop you instantly?

51
00:02:52,247 --> 00:02:53,336
That sounds safest.

52
00:02:53,995 --> 00:02:54,841
It is not.

53
00:02:56,246 --> 00:02:58,402
Picture an ordinary office elevator.

54
00:02:59,116 --> 00:03:01,816
Its brake kicks in at about two meters a second.

55
00:03:02,517 --> 00:03:05,164
Now stop that speed in just two centimeters.

56
00:03:05,932 --> 00:03:08,731
You would slow at about ten times the pull of gravity.

57
00:03:09,497 --> 00:03:11,968
Every person inside takes that jolt.

58
00:03:13,292 --> 00:03:16,148
The first famous safety brake did exactly that.

59
00:03:16,887 --> 00:03:23,123
In eighteen fifty-four, in New York, Elisha Otis stood on a platform high in the air and had its rope cut.

60
00:03:23,845 --> 00:03:28,636
Springs threw hooks into teeth along the guides, and the platform stopped where it was.

61
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:31,358
On a slow machine, that works.

62
00:03:32,022 --> 00:03:34,031
That kind of grab is still allowed today.

63
00:03:34,785 --> 00:03:36,881
But only on slow elevators.

64
00:03:37,434 --> 00:03:40,000
Sixty-three centimeters a second, or slower.

65
00:03:41,094 --> 00:03:42,370
And on anything faster?

66
00:03:43,292 --> 00:03:44,990
The rules ask for the opposite.

67
00:03:45,595 --> 00:03:47,015
Slow down steadily.

68
00:03:47,647 --> 00:03:49,709
Not too gently, and not too hard.

69
00:03:50,421 --> 00:03:54,324
On average, somewhere between a fifth of gravity and one full gravity.

70
00:03:55,109 --> 00:03:56,391
And what does that feel like?

71
00:03:57,362 --> 00:04:00,681
At the hardest, about twice your own weight, for a moment.

72
00:04:02,043 --> 00:04:04,731
So how do you stop a wedge from biting too hard?

73
00:04:05,635 --> 00:04:08,256
You give it a limit, and the limit is a spring.

74
00:04:09,173 --> 00:04:11,416
The jaws that hold the wedge are not rigid.

75
00:04:12,092 --> 00:04:15,522
Behind them, stacks of disc springs let the jaws open a little.

76
00:04:16,310 --> 00:04:21,796
The wedge climbs only so far, and from then on, the springs decide how hard the jaws squeeze.

77
00:04:23,163 --> 00:04:27,700
A steady squeeze means steady friction, and that means fairly steady slowing.

78
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,192
So your car slides down the rail with the wedges biting.

79
00:04:31,988 --> 00:04:38,082
Depending on how the springs are set, and how full the car is, you stop somewhere between twenty centimeters and a meter.

80
00:04:39,198 --> 00:04:40,523
Is it gentle on the rail?

81
00:04:41,423 --> 00:04:42,273
Not at all.

82
00:04:42,889 --> 00:04:44,935
The wedges actually scrape the rail.

83
00:04:45,705 --> 00:04:50,890
One maker's manual says the braking is friction, plus steel being shaved from the rail.

84
00:04:51,678 --> 00:04:53,801
Afterward, the marks are ground smooth.

85
00:04:54,609 --> 00:04:59,941
The car can only be released by raising it, and a trained person must check it before it carries you again.

86
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:03,857
So the wedge grabs, and the spring holds it back.

87
00:05:04,443 --> 00:05:06,745
That is the brake that is built to slip.

88
00:05:08,098 --> 00:05:10,234
Now, the danger I promised you.

89
00:05:11,039 --> 00:05:13,182
All of this only works one way.

90
00:05:14,007 --> 00:05:14,505
Down.

91
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:20,471
An elevator like yours is balanced by a heavy counterweight on the other side of its ropes.

92
00:05:21,218 --> 00:05:24,530
You never see it, but every time you go up, it comes down.

93
00:05:25,408 --> 00:05:31,285
To save energy, it usually weighs as much as the car plus about forty to fifty percent of a full load.

94
00:05:32,609 --> 00:05:34,163
Now imagine the car is empty.

95
00:05:34,948 --> 00:05:36,182
Which side is lighter now?

96
00:05:37,191 --> 00:05:37,810
The car.

97
00:05:38,727 --> 00:05:41,405
Suppose the machine's brake or its controls fail.

98
00:05:42,202 --> 00:05:47,879
The counterweight can drop and pull the empty car up, faster and faster, toward the top of the shaft.

99
00:05:49,123 --> 00:05:50,566
And the wedges you just watched?

100
00:05:51,369 --> 00:05:52,444
They cannot help.

101
00:05:53,026 --> 00:05:54,677
They only grip going down.

102
00:05:55,494 --> 00:05:56,744
Does that really happen?

103
00:05:57,726 --> 00:05:58,497
It does.

104
00:05:59,235 --> 00:06:02,096
One study looked at elevators in United States mines.

105
00:06:02,765 --> 00:06:06,578
In just five years, at least eighteen hit the top of their shafts.

106
00:06:07,776 --> 00:06:10,274
So the codes added a brake that faces up.

107
00:06:11,018 --> 00:06:13,136
Canada made it a rule in nineteen ninety.

108
00:06:13,730 --> 00:06:15,041
Europe requires it too.

109
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:21,288
Often the same governor now watches for speed in both directions, and sets off something that grips.

110
00:06:21,904 --> 00:06:26,458
For example, a clamp on the ropes themselves, or wedges that grip both ways.

111
00:06:27,828 --> 00:06:32,238
So the next time the doors close and the numbers climb, give it a second thought.

112
00:06:32,982 --> 00:06:35,604
Picture what is riding with you, out of sight.

113
00:06:36,431 --> 00:06:39,522
A thin rope you will never see, running over a wheel.

114
00:06:40,357 --> 00:06:43,069
Wedges, waiting just clear of the rails.

115
00:06:43,748 --> 00:06:47,184
And springs, set to stop you without stopping you dead.

116
00:06:48,327 --> 00:06:51,251
The safest brake is not the one that grabs the hardest.

117
00:06:51,866 --> 00:06:54,057
It is the one that grabs just hard enough.
